1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:06,065
In 79 AD, Vesuvius carbonized a library. The scrolls can't be opened, only scanned.

2
00:00:06,115 --> 00:00:11,483
To read one, you follow a single papyrus sheet through thousands of CT slices,

3
00:00:11,533 --> 00:00:15,094
where every sheet is crushed against its neighbours.

4
00:00:16,245 --> 00:00:19,829
Everything in this video was built during the hackathon,

5
00:00:19,879 --> 00:00:25,410
except SAM 3.1, the Muse models, the 2023 Grand Prize ink model and the Vesuvius data,

6
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:30,991
which I'll name when they appear. And to be clear: Unscroll has not read any new text.

7
00:00:31,041 --> 00:00:34,690
It measures how close an AI trace gets to being readable.

8
00:00:35,840 --> 00:00:41,429
I click one sheet, and SAM 3.1 follows it through the slices like an object in a video.

9
00:00:41,479 --> 00:00:46,809
Pink is the sheet a person traced by hand. These are two early traces from day two:

10
00:00:46,859 --> 00:00:51,476
orange slides further and further off, and teal is closer but still off.

11
00:00:51,526 --> 00:00:55,560
The meter turns teal only within 40 microns, about half a hair.

12
00:00:56,710 --> 00:01:01,644
The cause was the first slice: it took the brightest path through SAM's mask,

13
00:01:01,694 --> 00:01:04,297
which can jump to the neighbouring sheet.

14
00:01:04,347 --> 00:01:08,634
Unscroll's fix walks outward from the click instead. On this click:

15
00:01:08,684 --> 00:01:10,965
23 voxels off before, under 2 after.

16
00:01:12,115 --> 00:01:16,377
We tested it on 74 clicks we never used while building the fix.

17
00:01:16,427 --> 00:01:21,442
The share of the trace within 40 microns of the hand-traced sheet doubles,

18
00:01:21,492 --> 00:01:25,276
from 25 to 50 percent: better on 50 clicks, worse on 19.

19
00:01:25,326 --> 00:01:29,315
Against a classic optical-flow tracer, the gain is smaller,

20
00:01:29,365 --> 00:01:35,065
four and a half points, and on our 15 development clicks it's a tie. We report both.

21
00:01:36,215 --> 00:01:39,326
When a trace stops, a Muse Wonder agent takes over.

22
00:01:39,376 --> 00:01:42,487
It calls tools to re-seed from the last good slice,

23
00:01:42,537 --> 00:01:47,260
launch GPU runs on Kaggle and score the result. Then it checks its own seams:

24
00:01:47,310 --> 00:01:50,794
continuity numbers, plus Muse Spark looking at the image.

25
00:01:50,844 --> 00:01:54,389
Here it caught its first re-seed jumping to another sheet,

26
00:01:54,439 --> 00:01:58,295
threw out that run and the two built on it, and wrote down why.

27
00:01:59,445 --> 00:02:04,524
Why does staying on the sheet matter? Ink here is invisible to the eye;

28
00:02:04,574 --> 00:02:07,992
the Grand Prize model finds it from the surface.

29
00:02:08,042 --> 00:02:13,773
If the surface is off by a steady five voxels, the ink survives: agreement 0.79.

30
00:02:13,823 --> 00:02:18,541
If it wobbles by five voxels from slice to slice, it's gone: 0.01.

31
00:02:18,591 --> 00:02:21,215
So the enemy is wobble, not distance.

32
00:02:22,365 --> 00:02:27,190
So we smooth our own trace across slices. On a sheet with known text,

33
00:02:27,240 --> 00:02:31,429
agreement with the official ink map rises from 0.21 to 0.73,

34
00:02:31,479 --> 00:02:34,538
close to the 0.83 the official surface gets.

35
00:02:34,588 --> 00:02:41,251
But the maps show broad strokes, not letters, and fewer scholar clicks cover less of the sheet.

36
00:02:41,301 --> 00:02:43,795
That's the measured gap that's left.

37
00:02:44,945 --> 00:02:45,478
Unscroll:

38
00:02:45,528 --> 00:02:50,858
click a sheet, see whether the AI stayed on it, and see how far it is from reading.

39
00:02:50,908 --> 00:02:53,905
The code, the data and every number are public.
